---
// src/pages/share/wyoming-voters-choose/sources.astro
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../../components/Footer.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Sources: Wyoming Voters Should Choose -- Skovgard 2026"
  description="Primary sources supporting the Wyoming Voters Should Choose message: the Wyoming GOP bylaw dispute, pre-primary endorsements, and Wyoming election law W.S. 22-25-104."
>
  <Nav />
  <main class="pt-20">

    <!-- Breadcrumb bar -->
    <div class="bg-wy-bone border-b border-gray-200 py-3 px-4">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto flex items-center gap-2 flex-wrap text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest">
        <a href="/share" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-wy-ember transition-colors">All messages</a>
        <span class="text-gray-300">/</span>
        <a href="/share/wyoming-voters-choose/" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-wy-ember transition-colors">Wyoming Voters Should Choose</a>
        <span class="text-gray-300">/</span>
        <span class="text-wy-charcoal">Sources</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Header -->
    <div class="bg-wy-charcoal py-14 px-4 text-center">
      <p class="text-wy-sandstone font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-xs mb-3">Research Notes</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-serif font-extrabold text-wy-bone mb-4 leading-tight">
        Wyoming Voters Should Choose
      </h1>
      <p class="text-wy-bone/70 text-lg max-w-xl mx-auto">
        Sources supporting the claims in the share message.
        This is a research aid, not a legal opinion.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Preamble -->
    <section class="bg-wy-bone border-b border-gray-200 py-10 px-4">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl px-6 py-5 space-y-3">
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
            <strong>About this page.</strong> This page documents the public sources behind the
            Wyoming Voters Should Choose message. The message argues for voter power, local control,
            and primary elections decided by voters before party money or party machinery favors one
            candidate over another in a contested primary.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
            <strong>Important limitation.</strong> At the time this page was prepared, a final adopted
            2026 Wyoming GOP bylaws PDF confirming the exact final text had not been located. For that
            reason, the bylaw text below is described as reported bylaw language. The official adopted
            bylaws PDF remains the highest-priority source to add once available. Until then, this page
            relies on public reporting, the official convention page, Wyoming election law, and local
            commentary shared with the campaign.
          </p>
          <a href="/files/wyoming-voters-choose-sources.pdf"
             class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-sm font-semibold text-wy-ember hover:text-wy-ember-dark transition-colors"
             target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            <svg class="w-4 h-4 shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2"
                d="M12 10v6m0 0l-3-3m3 3l3-3m2 8H7a2 2 0 01-2-2V5a2 2 0 012-2h5.586a1 1 0 01.707.293l5.414 5.414a1 1 0 01.293.707V19a2 2 0 01-2 2z" />
            </svg>
            Download source packet (PDF)
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Main content -->
    <article class="bg-wy-bone py-12 px-4">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto sources-content">

        <!-- Core issue -->
        <h2>Core issue</h2>
        <p class="text-base text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          The share page argues that a primary election should belong to the voters. Party leaders,
          county committees, candidates, and advocacy groups can speak and organize, but the key
          public concern is whether party money and party machinery should favor one candidate over
          another before primary voters decide.
        </p>

        <!-- Reported bylaw language -->
        <h2>Reported bylaw language at issue</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          The Community Chatter opinion text attributes the following language to a bylaw change
          presented by Weston County. Cowboy State Daily reported similar proposed language before
          the convention. WyoFile later reported that the party adopted bylaws establishing a
          pre-primary vetting, endorsement, and financial-backing framework.
        </p>
        <blockquote class="bylaw-block">
          "The Wyoming Republican Party, through its State and County Central Committees, reserves
          the absolute First Amendment right to endorse, support, oppose, and provide financial
          assistance to candidates of its choosing at any point in the election cycle, including
          prior to a primary election. The Party's decisions regarding the use of its private funds
          and the content of its political speech are core associational activities. Any state law
          or administrative rule purporting to limit, restrict or prohibit this private advocacy is
          hereby declared unconstitutional and void."
        </blockquote>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          Source: Reported bylaw language. Use as reported language until the final adopted 2026
          Wyoming GOP bylaws PDF is added.
        </p>

        <!-- Wyoming law -->
        <h2>Wyoming law at issue</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          The relevant Wyoming statute is <strong>W.S. 22-25-104</strong>, titled
          <em>Restriction on party funds in primary elections</em>. Both the Wyoming Secretary of
          State Election Code PDF and the Wyoming Legislature Title 22 PDF provide the statute text.
        </p>
        <blockquote class="bylaw-block" style="border-left-color:#0f2742;">
          "No political party funds shall be expended directly or indirectly in the aid of the
          nomination of any one person as against another person of the same political party running
          in the primary election."
        </blockquote>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          W.S. 22-25-104 -- Wyoming Secretary of State Election Code PDF;
          Wyoming Legislature Title 22 PDF
        </p>

        <!-- Timeline -->
        <h2>Public timeline and context</h2>
        <div class="timeline-list mb-10">
          <div class="timeline-item">
            <span class="timeline-date">April 23 to April 25, 2026</span>
            <p>
              The Wyoming GOP held its 2026 State Convention at the Wyoming State Fairgrounds in
              Douglas. The official convention page lists the convention agenda and includes the
              Report of the Bylaws Committee.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="timeline-item">
            <span class="timeline-date">April 23, 2026</span>
            <p>
              Cowboy State Daily reported that county party proposals sought major bylaw changes,
              including language about party authority to endorse, support, oppose, and financially
              assist candidates at any point in the election cycle.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="timeline-item">
            <span class="timeline-date">April 27 and April 28, 2026</span>
            <p>
              WyoFile reported that the party adopted bylaws creating a framework to vet, endorse,
              and financially back candidates before the primary election.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="timeline-item">
            <span class="timeline-date">May 20, 2026</span>
            <p>
              Community Chatter published an opinion by Shurie Scheel describing the Hot Springs
              County dispute, the bylaw language, and the concern that primary voters should decide
              before party leaders use party support to favor candidates.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Claims table -->
        <h2>Key claims and source support</h2>
        <div class="claims-table" role="table" aria-label="Claims and source support">
          <div class="claims-header" role="row">
            <span role="columnheader">Public claim</span>
            <span role="columnheader">Best source</span>
          </div>
          <div class="claims-row" role="row">
            <div role="cell">
              The Wyoming GOP held its 2026 State Convention April 23 to April 25, 2026, in Douglas,
              and the agenda included the Report of the Bylaws Committee.
            </div>
            <div role="cell">
              Wyoming GOP 2026 State Convention page. Official convention context.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="claims-row" role="row">
            <div role="cell">
              The party adopted bylaws establishing a framework to vet, endorse, and financially back
              candidates before the primary.
            </div>
            <div role="cell">
              WyoFile. Main public reporting source for the adopted action and the pre-primary
              endorsement framework.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="claims-row" role="row">
            <div role="cell">
              Reported bylaw language claims authority to endorse, support, oppose, and financially
              assist candidates at any point in the election cycle.
            </div>
            <div role="cell">
              Cowboy State Daily (proposed language before convention); Community Chatter / Shurie
              Scheel (quoted bylaw text). Use as reported language until the final adopted bylaw PDF
              is available.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="claims-row" role="row">
            <div role="cell">
              Wyoming law restricts party funds from aiding one same-party candidate over another in
              a primary.
            </div>
            <div role="cell">
              Wyoming Secretary of State Election Code PDF; Wyoming Legislature Title 22 PDF; Justia
              mirror. Use official sources first. Justia is a readable backup.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="claims-row" role="row">
            <div role="cell">
              Primary voters should decide before party money or party machinery favors one candidate.
            </div>
            <div role="cell">
              W.S. 22-25-104; WyoFile; Community Chatter / Shurie Scheel. Fair public synthesis of
              the legal dispute and local concern.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Source list -->
        <h2>Primary sources</h2>

        <div class="source-list">

          <div class="source-item">
            <p class="source-label">
              Wyoming GOP 2026 State Convention page
              <span class="source-desc">Official party page -- convention context and agenda</span>
            </p>
            <a href="https://www.wyoming.gop/wygop-2026-state-convention"
               target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="source-link">
              wyoming.gop/wygop-2026-state-convention
            </a>
            <p class="source-note">
              Use for convention timing, location, and agenda. The agenda includes the Report of
              the Bylaws Committee. This page alone does not provide the final adopted bylaw PDF.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="source-item">
            <p class="source-label">
              WyoFile -- Wyoming Republican Party plans to buck law and endorse candidates ahead of critical primary
              <span class="source-desc">Independent Wyoming news report -- adopted bylaw framework</span>
            </p>
            <a href="https://wyofile.com/wyoming-republican-party-plans-to-buck-law-and-endorse-candidates-ahead-of-critical-primary/"
               target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="source-link">
              wyofile.com -- Wyoming Republican Party plans to buck law...
            </a>
            <p class="source-note">
              Main public reporting source for the adopted action. WyoFile reported that the party
              adopted bylaws creating a framework to vet, endorse, and financially back candidates
              before the primary.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="source-item">
            <p class="source-label">
              Cowboy State Daily -- Wyoming GOP Considering Loyalty Tests For Candidates, Voiding State Laws
              <span class="source-desc">Independent Wyoming news report -- proposed bylaw language before convention</span>
            </p>
            <a href="https://cowboystatedaily.com/2026/04/22/wyoming-gop-considering-loyalty-tests-for-candidates-voiding-state-laws/"
               target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="source-link">
              cowboystatedaily.com -- Wyoming GOP Considering Loyalty Tests...
            </a>
            <p class="source-note">
              Use for the reported proposed bylaw language and the conflict with Wyoming election
              law. Published April 23, 2026, before the convention concluded.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="source-item">
            <p class="source-label">
              Wyoming Secretary of State: 2026 Election Code PDF
              <span class="source-desc">Official state election-law source -- W.S. 22-25-104</span>
            </p>
            <a href="https://sos.wyo.gov/forms/Publications/ElectionCode_8.5x11.pdf"
               target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="source-link">
              sos.wyo.gov -- Election Code PDF
            </a>
            <p class="source-note">
              Official source for W.S. 22-25-104, the statute restricting party funds in primary
              elections. Use this as the primary legal source.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="source-item">
            <p class="source-label">
              Wyoming Legislature: Title 22 PDF
              <span class="source-desc">Official state statutes source -- election laws including W.S. 22-25-104</span>
            </p>
            <a href="https://wyoleg.gov/statutes/compress/title22.pdf"
               target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="source-link">
              wyoleg.gov -- Title 22 PDF
            </a>
            <p class="source-note">
              Legislature-hosted source for Title 22. Use as a second official legal source or
              backup for the Secretary of State Election Code PDF.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="source-item">
            <p class="source-label">
              Justia: W.S. 22-25-104
              <span class="source-desc">Readable statute mirror -- Restriction on party funds in primary elections</span>
            </p>
            <a href="https://law.justia.com/codes/wyoming/title-22/chapter-25/section-22-25-104/"
               target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="source-link">
              law.justia.com -- WY Stat Section 22-25-104
            </a>
            <p class="source-note">
              Plain web version of the statute, useful for readers who want a simple webpage
              instead of a PDF. Use alongside an official Wyoming state source.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="source-item">
            <p class="source-label">
              Community Chatter / Shurie Scheel -- Here's the Truth Behind the Take Back Wyoming Shop Party
              <span class="source-desc">Local commentary -- Hot Springs County context and bylaw language</span>
            </p>
            <!-- TODO: Replace with the confirmed public Community Chatter URL once available -->
            <span class="source-link todo-link">
              TODO: Public URL not yet confirmed -- Jimmy to replace with verified Community Chatter link
            </span>
            <p class="source-note">
              Published May 20, 2026. Provides local Hot Springs County context, the bylaw language
              as quoted in the article, and the voter-power argument. Include once the public URL
              is confirmed.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="source-item">
            <p class="source-label">
              Wyoming GOP 2024 Bylaws page
              <span class="source-desc">Official party governing-docs background -- prior convention cycle</span>
            </p>
            <a href="https://www.wyoming.gop/post/wygop-2024-bylaws"
               target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="source-link">
              wyoming.gop/post/wygop-2024-bylaws
            </a>
            <p class="source-note">
              Shows that Wyoming GOP bylaws and governing documents have been published online in
              prior convention cycles. Use as background context and as a monitoring location for
              any 2026 bylaw upload. Not proof of the 2026 bylaw change.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="source-item">
            <p class="source-label">
              TODO: Official adopted 2026 Wyoming GOP bylaws PDF
              <span class="source-desc">Highest-priority outstanding source</span>
            </p>
            <!-- TODO: Replace with the official adopted 2026 Wyoming GOP bylaws PDF once located -->
            <span class="source-link todo-link">
              TODO: Official adopted 2026 Wyoming GOP bylaws PDF -- Jimmy to replace when available
            </span>
            <p class="source-note">
              The final adopted bylaw PDF confirming the exact language passed at the 2026 state
              convention had not been located at the time this page was prepared. This is the
              highest-priority source to add once available.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="source-item">
            <p class="source-label">
              Eu v. San Francisco County Democratic Central Committee, 489 U.S. 214 (1989)
              <span class="source-desc">U.S. Supreme Court background -- party endorsement and associational rights</span>
            </p>
            <a href="https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/489/214/"
               target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="source-link">
              supreme.justia.com -- Eu v. San Francisco County Democratic Central Committee
            </a>
            <p class="source-note">
              Legal background frequently raised in disputes about party endorsement bans and
              political party associational rights. Use only as background legal context. This
              page does not provide legal advice.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <!-- Notes for responsible use -->
        <h2>Notes for responsible use</h2>
        <ul class="responsible-list">
          <li>Describe the bylaw language as "reported bylaw language" until the official adopted 2026 Wyoming GOP bylaws PDF is located and confirmed.</li>
          <li>Use WyoFile as the main public source for the adopted action. Use Cowboy State Daily and Community Chatter for the bylaw text while the official document is outstanding.</li>
          <li>Use "appears to," "claims the right to," or "reserves the right to" when describing the bylaw in public statements until the final text is independently confirmed.</li>
          <li>W.S. 22-25-104 is a statute, not a court ruling. Describe it as Wyoming law restricting party funds -- not as a ruling that the bylaw is illegal.</li>
          <li>The Eu case provides legal background. Do not describe this dispute as settled law either way. Use it as context, not as a conclusion.</li>
          <li>Separate the claim that bylaws were adopted (WyoFile) from the claim about the exact language (Cowboy State Daily, Community Chatter). Both matter; they come from different sources.</li>
        </ul>

      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Back CTA -->
    <div class="bg-white py-12 px-4 border-t border-gray-200 text-center">
      <a href="/share/wyoming-voters-choose/"
         class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 bg-wy-ember text-wy-bone font-bold rounded-lg hover:bg-wy-ember-dark transition-colors text-sm">
        <svg class="w-4 h-4 shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
          <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 19l-7-7 7-7" />
        </svg>
        Back to Wyoming Voters Should Choose
      </a>
    </div>

  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>

<style>
  .sources-content { line-height: 1.75; color: #2b2b2b; }
  .sources-content h2 {
    font-family: 'Bitter', serif;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 1.375rem;
    color: #2b2b2b;
    margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem;
    padding-bottom: 0.5rem;
    border-bottom: 2px solid #c68a4a;
  }
  .sources-content h2:first-child { margin-top: 0; }

  .bylaw-block {
    background: #fefce8;
    border-left: 4px solid #c68a4a;
    border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
    padding: 16px 20px;
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: #2b2b2b;
    font-style: italic;
  }

  /* Timeline */
  .timeline-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0;
    border-left: 3px solid #c68a4a;
    padding-left: 20px;
    margin-left: 8px;
  }
  .timeline-item {
    position: relative;
    padding-bottom: 20px;
  }
  .timeline-item::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: -27px;
    top: 6px;
    width: 10px;
    height: 10px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: #c68a4a;
    border: 2px solid #f1ece1;
  }
  .timeline-item:last-child { padding-bottom: 0; }
  .timeline-date {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: #c68a4a;
    margin-bottom: 4px;
  }
  .timeline-item p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: #374151;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  /* Claims table */
  .claims-table {
    border: 1px solid #ddd8ce;
    border-radius: 10px;
    overflow: hidden;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }
  .claims-header {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 2fr 3fr;
    background: #2b2b2b;
    gap: 0;
  }
  .claims-header span {
    padding: 10px 16px;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    color: #f1ece1;
  }
  .claims-header span:first-child { border-right: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.12); }
  .claims-row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 2fr 3fr;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd8ce;
  }
  .claims-row:last-child { border-bottom: none; }
  .claims-row div {
    padding: 14px 16px;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: #2b2b2b;
  }
  .claims-row div:first-child {
    background: #f1ece1;
    border-right: 1px solid #ddd8ce;
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  .claims-row div:last-child { background: #fafaf8; }
  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .claims-header { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .claims-header span:first-child { border-right: none; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.12); }
    .claims-row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .claims-row div:first-child { border-right: none; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd8ce; }
  }

  /* Source list */
  .source-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1px;
    border: 1px solid #e5e2da;
    border-radius: 10px;
    overflow: hidden;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }
  .source-item {
    background: #fff;
    padding: 16px 20px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #e5e2da;
  }
  .source-item:last-child { border-bottom: none; }
  .source-label {
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: #0f2742;
    margin: 0 0 4px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1px;
  }
  .source-desc {
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: #6b7280;
  }
  .source-link {
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: #b22234;
    text-decoration: none;
    word-break: break-all;
    margin-bottom: 4px;
  }
  .source-link:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
  .todo-link {
    color: #b45309;
    font-style: italic;
    cursor: default;
  }
  .source-note {
    margin: 4px 0 0;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: #6b7280;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  /* Responsible use */
  .responsible-list {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 10px;
  }
  .responsible-list li {
    display: flex;
    gap: 10px;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: #374151;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  .responsible-list li::before {
    content: "•";
    color: #c68a4a;
    font-weight: 700;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    margin-top: 2px;
  }
</style>
